    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d381">

                <pb facs="0621" n="593" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v03/pg593"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">320/9–1754: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bohlen</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Department
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1954-09-17T22:00:00+03:00">September 17,
                            1954—10 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>397. Acting under instructions (Embtel 324)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d381-f1"/> Thai
                    minister saw Gromyko last week concerning Soviet support of candidacy Prince Wan
                    for UN GA Presidency and was given noncommittal answer that entire matter “still
                    under consideration”. Gromyko reportedly did not <pb facs="0622" n="594"
                        xml:id="pg594"/> mention SEATO only saying, in reply to Thai’s statement
                    that he wished to better relations between the two countries, that relations
                    “ought” to be based on mutual interests.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bohlen</hi>
                    </signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
